@topic #define
@keys define macro
 #define

 Defines a macro.

## Syntax

   #define NAME replacement
   #define NAME(parameters) replacement

## Description

 Each later occurrence of NAME is replaced by the replacement text
 (and scanned again for macros). With parameters it is a function-like
 macro: the name must be directly followed by ( and the macro is only
 replaced when it is called with arguments. Enclose parameters and the
 whole replacement in parentheses to avoid precedence surprises, and
 remember that an argument with side effects is evaluated once for
 every use. A macro can also be defined on the command line
 (-Dname=value) or in Options|Compiler|Code generation|Defines. In the
 replacement # turns a parameter into a string and ## joins two
 tokens: {Stringizing and token pasting}.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#define SIZE 8
#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))
#define MAX(a, b) ((a) > (b) ? (a) : (b))

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d %d %d\n", SIZE, SQUARE(SIZE + 1), MAX(3, SIZE));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#undef} {#ifdef} {Stringizing and token pasting} {Predefined macros}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #elif
@keys elif
 #elif

 Else-if in a conditional group.

## Syntax

   #elif constant-expression

## Description

 Tested only if the preceding #if, #ifdef, #ifndef or #elif of the
 group was false.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#define LEVEL 2

int main(void)
{
#if LEVEL == 1
    printf("one\n");
#elif LEVEL == 2
    printf("two\n");
#else
    printf("other\n");
#endif
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#if} {#else} {#endif}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #else
@keys else-directive
 #else

 Alternative of a conditional group.

## Syntax

   #else

## Description

 The lines after #else are compiled if all earlier tests of the group
 were false. A group can have only one #else and it must come last.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
#ifdef __x86_64__
    printf("64-bit x86\n");
#else
    printf("something else\n");
#endif
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#if} {#elif} {#endif}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #endif
@keys endif
 #endif

 Ends a conditional group.

## Syntax

   #endif

## Description

 Every #if, #ifdef and #ifndef needs a matching #endif in the same
 file; a missing one is reported at the end of the file.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#if 0
    this text is skipped and never compiled
#endif

int main(void)
{
    printf("ok\n");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#if} {#ifdef} {#ifndef}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #error
@keys error-directive
 #error

 Stops the compilation with a message.

## Syntax

   #error text

## Description

 The text becomes the compiler message ('Error file line: #error
 directive: text'). Put it into a branch that must not be compiled to
 give a clear message for an unsupported configuration.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#ifndef __linux__
#error This program needs Linux
#endif

int main(void)
{
    printf("built for Linux\n");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#if} {Preprocessor}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #if
@keys defined
 #if

 Compiles the following lines if an expression is true.

## Syntax

   #if constant-expression

## Description

 The expression is evaluated by the preprocessor with integer
 arithmetic. It may use integer constants, character constants, macros
 (undefined names count as 0), the operators of C, and defined(NAME).
 It must not contain sizeof, casts or floating-point numbers. The
 group ends at #elif, #else or #endif; conditional groups may be
 nested.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#define VERSION 3

int main(void)
{
#if VERSION >= 3 && defined(__linux__)
    printf("new version on Linux\n");
#else
    printf("old version\n");
#endif
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#elif} {#else} {#endif} {#ifdef} {#define}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #ifdef
@keys ifdef
 #ifdef

 Compiles the following lines if a macro is defined.

## Syntax

   #ifdef NAME

## Description

 Same as #if defined(NAME). Often used with a macro that is set from
 the command line (-DDEBUG) to switch debugging code on and off.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#define DEBUG

int main(void)
{
#ifdef DEBUG
    printf("debug build\n");
#endif
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#ifndef} {#if} {#endif} {#define}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #ifndef
@keys ifndef guard
 #ifndef

 Compiles the following lines if a macro is not defined.

## Syntax

   #ifndef NAME

## Description

 Same as #if !defined(NAME). The classic use is the include guard of a
 header file, which makes it harmless to include twice.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#ifndef LIMIT
#define LIMIT 10          /* default, can be set with -DLIMIT=20 */
#endif

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", LIMIT);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#ifdef} {#define} {#include}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #include
@keys include
 #include

 Inserts the text of another file.

## Syntax

   #include <file>
   #include "file"
   #include macro

## Description

 The <file> form searches the include directories
 (Options|Directories, fcc -I) and then the built-in headers; the
 "file" form searches the directory of the including file first.
 Includes may be nested. A header is read each time it is included, so
 guard your own headers with #ifndef. A macro that expands to either
 form can be used after #include.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", (int)strlen("include"));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#ifndef} {#define} {Preprocessor}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #line
@keys line
 #line

 Changes the line number and file name used in messages.

## Syntax

   #line number
   #line number "file"

## Description

 The next source line is treated as line number; __LINE__ and the
 compiler messages use it. With a file name __FILE__ changes too.
 Mostly used by programs that generate C source.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#line 500 "generated.c"

int main(void)
{
    printf("%s:%d\n", __FILE__, __LINE__);   /* generated.c:504 */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Predefined macros} {Preprocessor}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #pragma
@keys pragma startup
 #pragma

 Compiler-specific instructions.

## Syntax

   #pragma startup function [priority]
   #pragma exit function [priority]

## Description

 startup names a void function without parameters that runs before
 main, exit one that runs after main returns or exit() is called; the
 function has to be declared before the pragma. The priority 0-255
 (default 100) orders them: startup functions run from the lowest
 number up, exit functions from the highest down. Any other pragma is
 ignored.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

void begin(void) { printf("before main\n"); }
void finish(void) { printf("after main\n"); }

#pragma startup begin
#pragma exit finish

int main(void)
{
    printf("in main\n");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {main} {Libraries and linking} {atexit}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic #undef
@keys undef
 #undef

 Removes a macro definition.

## Syntax

   #undef NAME

## Description

 Removing a macro that is not defined is not an error. Useful to
 redefine a macro or to switch off a library macro. Macros can also be
 removed from the command line (-Uname) or in Options|Compiler|Code
 generation|Undefine symbols.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#define LEVEL 1
#undef LEVEL
#define LEVEL 2

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", LEVEL);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#define} {#ifdef}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Address and indirection operators
@keys & * address indirection dereference
 Address and indirection operators

 & (address of) and * (indirection)

## Syntax

   &lvalue
   *pointer

## Description

 &x is a pointer to x; it cannot be applied to a bit field or a
 register object. *p is the object p points to; it may be used on the
 left of an assignment. * and & undo each other: *&x is x.
 Dereferencing a null or invalid pointer ends the program with a
 segmentation fault.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

static void swap(int *a, int *b)
{
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void)
{
    int x = 1, y = 2;

    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Pointers} {Arrays} {Member access operators}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Arithmetic operators
@keys + - * / % arithmetic operator
 Arithmetic operators

 + - * / % and unary + -.

## Syntax

   a + b   a - b   a * b   a / b   a % b   +a   -a

## Description

 The usual conversions bring both operands to a common type first (see
 {Type conversions}). Integer division truncates toward zero; the
 result of % has the sign of the left operand. Division or remainder
 by zero stops the program with a run-time error; with a constant
 divisor the compiler warns 'Division by zero'. Integer overflow wraps
 around.

 Pointer arithmetic: pointer + n and pointer - n move by n elements,
 and pointer - pointer gives the number of elements between them as a
 long.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d %d\n", 7 / 2, -7 / 2);     /* 3 -3 */
    printf("%d %d\n", 7 % 3, -7 % 3);     /* 1 -1 */
    printf("%.2f\n", 7 / 2.0);             /* 3.50 */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Operator precedence} {Assignment operators} {Pointers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Arrays
@keys array arrays subscript
 Arrays

 Fixed-size sequences of elements.

## Syntax

   type name[size];
   type name[] = { value, ... };
   type name[rows][columns];

## Description

 Elements are numbered from 0 and stored one after the other; there is
 no range checking. The size must be a constant (variable-length
 arrays are not supported). In most expressions an array turns into a
 pointer to its first element, so arrays are passed to functions as
 pointers and the callee cannot find the size. The exceptions are
 sizeof and &. Multi-dimensional arrays are arrays of arrays, stored
 row by row.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int grid[2][3] = { { 1, 2, 3 }, { 4, 5, 6 } };
    int total = 0, r, c;

    for (r = 0; r < 2; r++)
        for (c = 0; c < 3; c++)
            total += grid[r][c];
    printf("total %d, %d elements\n", total,
        (int)(sizeof grid / sizeof(int)));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Pointers} {Initializers} {sizeof} {Strings}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Assignment operators
@keys = += -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<= >>= assignment
 Assignment operators

 = += -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<= >>=

## Syntax

   lvalue = expression
   lvalue op= expression

## Description

 The value of the expression is converted to the type of the left side
 and stored. An assignment is itself an expression whose value is the
 stored value, so a = b = 0 works. a op= b means a = a op b except
 that a is evaluated only once. Assigning to a const object is an
 error.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a, b, c = 10;

    a = b = 3;               /* both are 3 */
    c += a * b;              /* c = c + a * b */
    c <<= 1;
    printf("%d %d %d\n", a, b, c);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Increment and decrement} {Arithmetic operators} {const}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Bit fields
@keys bitfield bit-field bits
 Bit fields

 Structure members with a given number of bits.

## Syntax

   unsigned name : width;
   int name : width;
   int : width;     /* padding */
   int : 0;         /* start a new unit */

## Description

 Bit fields are members of type int, signed int or unsigned int with a
 constant width of 1 to 32 bits. A plain int field is signed. Fields
 are packed from the least significant bit into 32-bit units and never
 straddle a unit. A field can be used like any integer lvalue, but &
 and sizeof cannot be applied to it. char, short and long fields are
 errors.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

struct flags {
    unsigned ready : 1;
    unsigned mode  : 3;
    int      delta : 4;      /* signed: -8 .. 7 */
};

int main(void)
{
    struct flags f = { 1, 5, -3 };

    f.mode++;
    printf("%d %d %d (size %d)\n", f.ready, f.mode, f.delta,
        (int)sizeof f);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {struct} {union}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Bitwise and shift operators
@keys & | ^ ~ << >> bitwise shift
 Bitwise and shift operators

 & | ^ ~ << >>

## Syntax

   a & b   a | b   a ^ b   ~a   a << n   a >> n

## Description

 & | ^ work on every bit of the operands, ~ inverts all bits. a << n
 shifts left, filling with zeros; a >> n shifts right, and for a
 signed negative value it keeps the sign bit (arithmetic shift). The
 shift count must be smaller than the width of the left operand. The
 result type is the promoted type of the left operand.

 The warning 'Ambiguous operators need parentheses' (-wamb) points at
 expressions such as a & b | c or a << b + c.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    unsigned flags = 0;

    flags |= 1 << 3;         /* set bit 3 */
    flags |= 1 << 0;
    flags &= ~1u;            /* clear bit 0 */
    printf("flags = 0x%x, bit 3 is %s\n", flags,
        (flags & (1 << 3)) ? "set" : "clear");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Relational and logical operators} {Assignment operators}
 {Operator precedence}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Cast operator
@keys cast
 Cast operator

 (type) expression

## Syntax

   (type) expression

## Description

 Converts the value of the expression to the type. Casts are used to
 force floating-point division, to convert between pointer types and
 to silence the pointer conversion warnings. A cast does not make an
 lvalue. The cast to void discards a value.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a = 7, b = 2;

    printf("%d\n", a / b);                  /* 3 */
    printf("%.2f\n", (double)a / b);        /* 3.50 */
    printf("%d\n", (int)3.99);               /* 3 */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Type conversions} {sizeof} {Pointers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Character constants and escape sequences
@keys escape character constant \n \t \0
 Character constants and escape sequences

 'a', '\n', '\x41' and L'a'.

## Syntax

   'c'   '\n'   '\012'   '\x1B'   L'c'

## Description

 A character constant has type int. Escape sequences:

   \n new-line      \t tab            \v vertical tab
   \b backspace     \r return         \f form feed
   \a alert         \\ backslash      \' quote
   \" double quote  \? question mark
   \ooo octal (up to 3 digits)   \xhh hexadecimal

 The same escapes are used in string literals. A wide character
 constant L'x' has type wchar_t (32-bit int).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("tab:\there\n");
    printf("%c%c%c\n", 'A', '\x42', '\103');   /* ABC */
    printf("quote: \"%c\" bell:\a\n", '\'');
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Strings} {char} {Integer constants}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Comma operator
@keys , comma
 Comma operator

 expression1 , expression2

## Syntax

   expression , expression

## Description

 Evaluates the left expression, discards it, then evaluates the right
 one and yields its value. It is mostly used in the parts of a for
 statement. In argument lists and declarations the comma is only a
 separator; use parentheses to get the operator: f((a, b)).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i, j;

    for (i = 0, j = 10; i < j; i++, j--)
        ;
    printf("%d %d\n", i, j);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {for} {Operator precedence}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Comments
@keys comment /* //
 Comments

 /* ... */ and // ... comments.

## Syntax

   /* comment */
   // comment to the end of the line

## Description

 A /* comment */ may span lines and is replaced by one space. Comments
 do not nest unless Options|Compiler|Source|Nested comments is on (fcc
 -C). A // comment runs to the end of the line; it is a C99 extension
 and is refused with Keywords set to ANSI (fcc -A). To disable a block
 of code that contains comments use #if 0 ... #endif.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* a block comment
       over two lines */
    printf("one\n");   // a line comment
#if 0
    printf("this is compiled out\n");
#endif
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#if} {Preprocessor}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Conditional operator
@keys ?: ? ternary conditional
 Conditional operator

 expression ? a : b

## Syntax

   condition ? expression1 : expression2

## Description

 Only one of the two result expressions is evaluated. Their types are
 brought to a common type. The operator has low precedence and groups
 from right to left, so a ? b : c ? d : e means a ? b : (c ? d : e).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n;

    for (n = 0; n < 3; n++)
        printf("%d item%s\n", n, n == 1 ? "" : "s");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {if} {else} {Operator precedence}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Data types
@keys types size ranges
 Data types

 The types of Flash C and their sizes.

## Description

   char                 1 byte      -128 .. 127 (or 0 .. 255 with -K)
   short                2 bytes     -32768 .. 32767
   int                  4 bytes     -2147483648 .. 2147483647
   long                 8 bytes     64-bit
   unsigned versions    same sizes, 0 .. 2^N-1
   float                4 bytes     about 7 digits
   double               8 bytes     about 15 digits
   pointer              8 bytes
   enum                 as int (or the smallest fitting type with -b-)

 long long is long, long double is double. Compare with DOS compilers,
 where int and pointers were 16 bits: code that depends on that size
 has to be reviewed.

 Derived types are pointers, arrays, functions, structures and unions.
 wchar_t is a 32-bit int.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("char %d, short %d, int %d, long %d\n",
        (int)sizeof(char), (int)sizeof(short), (int)sizeof(int),
            (int)sizeof(long));
    printf("float %d, double %d, pointer %d\n",
        (int)sizeof(float), (int)sizeof(double), (int)sizeof(void *));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Integer types} {Floating constants} {char} {int} {long} {double}
 {struct} {sizeof}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Declarations
@keys declaration declarator
 Declarations

 How variables, functions and types are declared.

## Syntax

   storage-class qualifier type declarator = initializer, ... ;

## Description

 A declaration names a type and one or more declarators. Declarators
 combine * (pointer to), [] (array of) and () (function returning) and
 are read from the name outward:

   int *p;          p is a pointer to int
   int a[10];       a is an array of 10 int
   int *a[10];      a is an array of 10 pointers to int
   int (*a)[10];    a is a pointer to an array of 10 int
   int f(void);     f is a function returning int
   int (*f)(void);  f is a pointer to a function returning int

 Declarations may appear anywhere in a block, not only at its start.
 Use typedef to give complicated types a name. The warning "'x' is
 declared but never used" is available (-wuse).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a = 1, *p = &a, v[3] = { 4, 5, 6 };
    int (*pv)[3] = &v;

    printf("%d %d\n", *p, (*pv)[2]);
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)       /* declaration inside for */
        printf("%d ", v[i]);
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Storage classes and scope} {typedef} {Pointers} {Arrays}
 {Function pointers} {Initializers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Expressions
@keys expression lvalue
 Expressions

 Operands, operators and their results.

## Description

 An expression is built from constants, variables, function calls and
 operators. An lvalue is an expression that designates an object (a
 variable, *p, a[i], s.m, p->m) and can be assigned to or have its
 address taken. The order of evaluation of operands and of function
 arguments is unspecified, so avoid side effects that depend on it. An
 expression followed by a semicolon is a statement; the warning 'Code
 has no effect' (-weff) catches expression statements that do nothing.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a = 3, b = 4;
    int hyp2 = a * a + b * b;

    printf("%d\n", hyp2);
    printf("%d\n",
        (a = 10) + b); /* an assignment is an expression */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Operator precedence} {Arithmetic operators} {Assignment operators}
 {Type conversions} {Statements}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Extended keywords
@keys near far huge cdecl pascal interrupt _near _far _huge
 Extended keywords

 near, far, huge, cdecl, pascal and interrupt.

## Syntax

   type near name;      type far *p;
   int cdecl f(void);    void interrupt handler();

## Description

 These keywords come from 16-bit DOS compilers, where they selected
 pointer sizes, calling conventions and interrupt handlers. Flash C
 produces flat 64-bit code, so the keywords are accepted and ignored:
 an existing program that uses them still compiles, but they change
 nothing.

 With Options|Compiler|Source|Keywords set to ANSI (fcc -A) they are
 not keywords at all and can be used as ordinary identifiers, which is
 what strict ANSI C requires.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int cdecl twice(int x)       /* cdecl is ignored */
{
    return x * 2;
}

int main(void)
{
    char far *p = "far pointers are plain pointers";

    printf("%s %d\n", p, twice(21));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Not supported DOS features} {Differences from classic compilers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Floating constants
@keys floating real exponent
 Floating constants

 Numbers with a decimal point or exponent.

## Syntax

   3.14   .5   2.   1e10   1.5E-3   2.5f   1.0L

## Description

 A floating constant has type double unless it ends in f or F (float)
 or l or L (long double, the same as double). The exponent is e or E
 followed by an optionally signed decimal number. Floating point is
 not allowed in #if expressions.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    double a = 1.5e3, b = .25;
    float c = 0.1f;

    printf("%g %g %g\n", a, b, c);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Integer constants} {double} {float}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Function pointers
@keys callback function-pointer
 Function pointers

 Pointers to functions.

## Syntax

   type (*name)(parameter-list);
   name = function;   name(args)   (*name)(args)

## Description

 The name of a function converts to a pointer to it. A function
 pointer is called like a function. They are used for callbacks
 (qsort, bsearch, atexit, signal) and tables of handlers. Use typedef
 to make the declarations readable.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
static int mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }

int main(void)
{
    int (*op[2])(int, int) = { add, mul };
    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < 2; i++)
        printf("%d\n", op[i](6, 7));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {typedef} {qsort} {Functions} {Pointers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Functions
@keys function prototype parameter argument call
 Functions

 Defining, declaring and calling functions.

## Syntax

   type name(parameter-list) { body }
   type name(parameter-list);       /* prototype */
   name(arguments)

## Description

 A prototype declares the parameter types so that arguments are
 checked and converted; call a function that has no prototype and you
 get 'Call to function with no prototype' (-wpro) and the arguments
 are only promoted. Put prototypes in headers and include them.
 Parameters are passed by value; pass a pointer to let the function
 change the caller's variable. Structures are passed and returned by
 value. Arrays are passed as pointers. Recursion is allowed.

 A parameter list ending in ... takes extra arguments: see
 {Variable arguments}. The parameter list (void) means none. A
 function has to be defined once in the program.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

static int fact(int n);               /* prototype */

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", fact(5));
    return 0;
}

static int fact(int n)
{
    return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1);
}
@end

## See also

 {return} {void} {main} {Function pointers} {Variable arguments}
 {static} {extern}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Identifiers
@keys identifier name
 Identifiers

 Names of variables, functions, types and labels.

## Description

 An identifier starts with a letter or underscore and continues with
 letters, digits and underscores. Case is significant.
 Options|Compiler|Source|Identifier Length (fcc -iN) sets how many
 leading characters are significant; the default of 32 is also the
 maximum. Names that start with two underscores are reserved for the
 compiler and library. Keywords cannot be used as identifiers.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int count = 1, Count = 2,
        _count = 3; /* three different names */

    printf("%d\n", count + Count + _count);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Keywords and operators} {Declarations}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Increment and decrement
@keys ++ -- increment decrement
 Increment and decrement

 ++ and --

## Syntax

   ++lvalue   --lvalue   lvalue++   lvalue--

## Description

 The prefix form changes the object and yields the new value, the
 postfix form yields the old value. On a pointer the step is one
 element. Do not change the same object twice in one expression (i =
 i++ is undefined).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i = 5;
    int a = i++;             /* a = 5, i = 6 */
    int b = ++i;             /* i = 7, b = 7 */

    printf("%d %d %d\n", a, b, i);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Assignment operators} {Pointers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Initializers
@keys initializer initialiser initialization
 Initializers

 Giving an object a value when it is declared.

## Syntax

   type name = expression;
   type name[] = { value, value, ... };
   struct tag name = { value, ... };
   type name[] = { [2] = 5 };   struct tag name = { .member = value };

## Description

 Static and global objects need constant initialisers and are zero
 when there is none. Automatic objects may use any expression. Braces
 may be nested for arrays of arrays and structures, and missing
 trailing values are zero. A char array can be initialised with a
 string; when its size is left out it is taken from the initialiser.
 Designated initialisers ([index] = and .member =) are accepted as a
 C99 convenience.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

struct pt { int x, y, z; };

int main(void)
{
    int a[5] = { 1, 2 };                 /* 1 2 0 0 0 */
    char s[] = "hello";                  /* 6 bytes */
    struct pt p = { .y = 7, .x = 1 };    /* z is 0 */

    printf("%d %d %d %d\n", a[1], a[4], (int)sizeof s, p.y);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Arrays} {struct} {Strings} {Declarations}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Integer constants
@keys constant octal hexadecimal 0x
 Integer constants

 Decimal, octal and hexadecimal numbers.

## Syntax

   123   0777   0x1F   123u   123L   123UL

## Description

 A constant that starts with 0 is octal, with 0x or 0X is hexadecimal,
 otherwise decimal. The suffix u makes it unsigned, l makes it long;
 they can be combined. Without a suffix the constant has the first of
 int, unsigned int (hex and octal only), long, unsigned long in which
 it fits. Digits 8 and 9 in an octal constant are an error.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d %d %d\n", 255, 0377, 0xFF);   /* all 255 */
    printf("%lu\n", 4294967296UL);
    printf("%d\n", (int)sizeof 3000000000);  /* does not fit int: 8 */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Floating constants} {Character constants and escape sequences}
 {Integer types}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Integer types
@keys limits.h integer INT_MAX
 Integer types

 char, short, int and long with their limits.

## Description

 The ranges are in <limits.h>: CHAR_BIT, CHAR_MIN, CHAR_MAX,
 SCHAR_MIN, SCHAR_MAX, UCHAR_MAX, SHRT_MIN, SHRT_MAX, USHRT_MAX,
 INT_MIN, INT_MAX, UINT_MAX, LONG_MIN, LONG_MAX, ULONG_MAX.

 Signed overflow and unsigned overflow both wrap around in the
 generated code. A conversion to a smaller type keeps the low bits.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("short %d..%d\n", SHRT_MIN, SHRT_MAX);
    printf("int   %d..%d\n", INT_MIN, INT_MAX);
    printf("long  %ld..%ld\n", LONG_MIN, LONG_MAX);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Data types} {Type conversions} {Integer constants} {char} {short}
 {int} {long} {unsigned}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Keywords and operators
@keys keywords operators
@alias C keywords
 Keywords and operators

## Keywords

  {auto} {break} {case} {char} {const} {continue} {default} {do}
  {double} {else} {enum} {extern} {float} {for} {goto} {if} {int} {long}
  {register} {return} {short} {signed} {sizeof} {static} {struct}
  {switch} {typedef} {union} {unsigned} {void} {volatile} {while}
  {Extended keywords}

 Operators, highest precedence first

   () [] -> .                       left to right
   ! ~ ++ -- + - * & (type) sizeof  right to left
   * / %                            left to right
   + -
   << >>
   < <= > >=
   == !=
   &    ^    |
   &&   ||
   ?:                               right to left
   = += -= *= /= %= &= ^= |= <<= >>=
   ,                                left to right

  {Arithmetic operators} {Relational and logical operators}
  {Bitwise and shift operators} {Assignment operators}
  {Increment and decrement} {Conditional operator} {Comma operator}
  {Address and indirection operators} {Member access operators}
  {Cast operator} {Operator precedence}

 See {Language topics}, {Preprocessor}, {Language reference}

