 See also: {Language reference}, {Library functions}, {Compiler options}

@topic Member access operators
@keys . -> [] member subscript
 Member access operators

 . -> and []

## Syntax

   structure.member
   pointer->member
   array[index]

## Description

 a.b selects a member of a structure or union object; p->b is the same
 as (*p).b. a[i] is *(a + i), so it also works with a pointer, and
 i[a] means the same. Subscripts are not range checked.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

struct node { int value; struct node *next; };

int main(void)
{
    struct node b = { 2, 0 };
    struct node a = { 1, &b };
    struct node *p;

    for (p = &a; p; p = p->next)
        printf("%d ", p->value);
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {struct} {union} {Arrays} {Address and indirection operators}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Operator precedence
@keys precedence associativity
 Operator precedence

 Order in which operators are applied.

## Description

 Highest precedence first; operators in one row have equal precedence.

   () [] -> .                          left to right
   ! ~ ++ -- + - * & (type) sizeof     right to left (unary)
   * / %                               left to right
   + -
   << >>
   < <= > >=
   == !=
   &
   ^
   |
   &&
   ||
   ?:                                  right to left
   = += -= *= /= %= &= ^= |= <<= >>=   right to left
   ,                                   left to right

 The order in which operands are evaluated is not defined, except for
 && || ?: and the comma. Add parentheses when in doubt; -wamb warns
 about the mixes people most often get wrong.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a = 2, b = 3, c = 4;

    printf("%d\n", a + b * c);          /* 14 */
    printf("%d\n", (a + b) * c);        /* 20 */
    printf("%d\n", a | b & c);          /* & first: 2 | 0 = 2 */
    printf("%d\n", a < b == 1);         /* (a < b) == 1 */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Arithmetic operators} {Bitwise and shift operators}
 {Keywords and operators}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Pointers
@keys pointer pointers NULL
 Pointers

 Variables that hold addresses.

## Syntax

   type *name;   *p   &x   p + n   p - q

## Description

 A pointer is 8 bytes. Pointer arithmetic is scaled by the size of the
 pointed-to type: p + 1 is the next element. Pointers of the same type
 can be compared and subtracted if they point into the same array. A
 null pointer (0, NULL) points nowhere and compares false. void *
 converts to and from any object pointer without a cast.

 Converting between integers and pointers is non-portable; the
 compiler warns 'Non-portable pointer conversion' (-wrpt) and for
 different pointee types 'Suspicious pointer conversion' (-wsus); char
 versus unsigned char gives 'Mixing pointers to signed and unsigned
 char' (-wucp).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int v[4] = { 10, 20, 30, 40 };
    int *p = v;

    p += 2;
    printf("%d %d %d\n", *p, p[1], (int)(p - v));   /* 30 40 2 */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Address and indirection operators} {Arrays} {Function pointers} {void}
 {const}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Predefined macros
@keys __FILE__ __LINE__ __DATE__ __TIME__ __STDC__
 Predefined macros

## Macros

   __FILE__   the name of the source file as a string
   __LINE__   the current line number
   __DATE__   the compilation date, "Mmm dd yyyy"
   __TIME__   the compilation time, "hh:mm:ss"
   __STDC__   1
   __FLASHC__ 0x0100, the compiler version
   __linux__  1
   __x86_64__ 1

 Add your own with {#define} or the -D switch. See {Preprocessor}

@topic Preprocessor
@keys preprocessor directive
@alias Preprocessor directives
 Preprocessor

## Directives

   #include <file>   search the include directories
   #include "file"   search the current directory first
   #define NAME text / #define NAME(args) text
   #undef  NAME
   #if expr / #ifdef / #ifndef / #elif / #else / #endif
   #line number "file"
   #error text
   #pragma ...       startup and exit functions, others ignored

 Operators in macros: # makes a string, ## joins tokens.
 Defined in expressions: defined(NAME).

 Each directive has its own topic:
  {#include} {#define} {#undef} {#if} {#ifdef} {#ifndef} {#elif} {#else}
  {#endif} {#line} {#error} {#pragma}
 {Stringizing and token pasting}

 See {Predefined macros}, {Language topics}

@topic Relational and logical operators
@keys && || ! == != <= >= relational logical
 Relational and logical operators

 < <= > >= == != && || !

## Syntax

   a < b  a <= b  a > b  a >= b  a == b  a != b
   a && b   a || b   !a

## Description

 Comparisons and logical operators produce the int 1 (true) or 0
 (false). Any nonzero value counts as true. && and || evaluate the
 left operand first and skip the right one when the result is known
 (short-circuit), so a pointer can be tested and used in one
 expression: p && p->next. Do not confuse == with =; the warning
 'Possibly incorrect assignment' catches if (a = b).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a = 5, b = 0;

    printf("%d %d %d\n", a > 3, a == b, !b);
    printf("%d\n",
        b != 0 && 10 / b > 1); /* no division: b != 0 is false */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {if} {while} {Bitwise and shift operators} {Operator precedence}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Statements
@keys statement block compound
 Statements

 The statements of C.

## Description

   expression;                  expression statement
   { declarations statements }  compound statement (block)
   ;                            empty statement
   {if} {else} {switch}              selection
   {while} {do} {for}               iteration
   {break} {continue} {goto} {return}   jumps
   label:  case n:  default:    labelled statements

 The warning 'Unreachable code' (-wrch) is given for a statement that
 follows return, break, continue or goto without a label in between.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i = 0;               /* declaration */

    i++;                     /* expression statement */
    {                        /* block */
        int j = i * 2;
        printf("%d\n", j);
    }
    ;                        /* empty statement */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Expressions} {Declarations}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Storage classes and scope
@keys scope storage linkage visibility
 Storage classes and scope

 Where a name is visible and how long it lives.

## Description

 Scope
   file       declared outside functions: visible to the end of the file
   function   labels only
   block      declared inside { }: visible to the end of the block
   prototype  parameter names in a prototype

 A name in an inner block hides the same name in an outer one.

 Storage
   automatic  locals and parameters (stack); keyword auto
   static     file-scope objects and static locals: exist for the whole
              run and start as zero
   external   extern: defined in another file

 Uninitialised automatic variables contain garbage; static and global
 objects are zeroed.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int x = 1;                   /* file scope */

int main(void)
{
    int x = 2;               /* hides the global */

    {
        int x = 3;           /* hides the one above */
        printf("%d\n", x);
    }
    printf("%d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {auto} {static} {extern} {register} {Declarations}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Stringizing and token pasting
@keys # ## stringize stringizing paste concatenation
 Stringizing and token pasting

 The # and ## operators in macros.

## Syntax

   #parameter
   token ## token

## Description

 In the replacement text of a function-like macro, #x turns the
 argument into a string literal (quotes and backslashes inside are
 escaped) and a ## b joins the two tokens into one, which is then
 scanned for macros. Arguments next to # or ## are not macro-expanded
 first; to expand them use an extra macro level.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

#define SHOW(e)   printf("%s = %d\n", #e, e)
#define VAR(n)    value ## n

int main(void)
{
    int value1 = 11, value2 = 22;

    SHOW(3 * 4);
    SHOW(VAR(2) + VAR(1));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {#define} {Preprocessor}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Strings
@keys string literal
 Strings

 String literals and character arrays.

## Syntax

   "text"   char name[] = "text";   char *p = "text";

## Description

 A string literal is an array of char ending with a zero byte, so
 "abc" occupies 4 bytes. Adjacent literals are joined: "ab" "cd" is
 "abcd". A character array initialised from a literal is a modifiable
 copy; a pointer initialised from a literal points at the literal
 itself, which should not be changed. With Options|Compiler|Code
 generation|Duplicate strings merged (fcc -d) identical literals share
 one copy. Wide strings are written L"text". <string.h> has the
 functions that work on strings.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void)
{
    char buf[32] = "Flash";
    const char *msg = "adjacent " "literals";

    strcat(buf, " C");
    printf("%s (%d chars, array %d bytes)\n", buf,
        (int)strlen(buf), (int)sizeof buf);
    puts(msg);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Library: string} {Arrays} {Character constants and escape sequences}
 {char}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Type conversions
@keys conversion promotion
 Type conversions

 Implicit and explicit conversions.

## Description

 Usual conversions for arithmetic and comparisons:
   1. char and short become int (integer promotion).
   2. If either operand is double (or float) the other becomes double.
   3. Otherwise if either is unsigned long both become unsigned long,
      else if either is long both become long.
   4. Otherwise if either is unsigned int both become unsigned int.
   5. Otherwise both are int.

 Assignment converts to the type of the left side; a conversion to a
 narrower integer keeps the low bits; floating to integer truncates
 toward zero. Argument passing without a prototype promotes float to
 double. A signed value mixed with an unsigned one of the same size is
 treated as unsigned. Use a cast to make a conversion explicit.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    char c = 100;
    int i = c * 3;           /* promoted to int: 300, no overflow */
    unsigned char u = i;     /* low 8 bits: 44 */
    int t = 3.99;            /* truncated: 3 */

    printf("%d %d %d\n", i, u, t);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Cast operator} {Integer types} {unsigned} {Functions}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Type qualifiers
@keys qualifier
 Type qualifiers

 const and volatile.

## Syntax

   const type name;   volatile type name;   const volatile type name;

## Description

 A qualifier applies to the type on its left (or, at the start of a
 declaration, to the base type). In const char *p the characters are
 read-only; in char *const p the pointer is read-only. See {const} and
 {volatile}.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    char text[] = "abc";
    const char *a = text;        /* cannot change *a */
    char *const b = text;        /* cannot change b */

    b[0] = 'X';
    printf("%s\n", a);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {const} {volatile} {Pointers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic Variable arguments
@keys stdarg ellipsis varargs
 Variable arguments

 Functions with a variable number of parameters.

## Syntax

   type name(type fixed, ...)
   va_list ap;  va_start(ap, fixed);  va_arg(ap, type);  va_end(ap);

## Description

 Include <stdarg.h>. The function needs at least one fixed parameter;
 va_start(ap, last) starts after it, va_arg(ap, type) returns the next
 argument and va_end(ap) finishes. Arguments without a declared type
 are promoted: char and short become int, float becomes double, so ask
 for int and double, never char or float. The function must know how
 many arguments there are, e.g. from a count or a format string.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>

static int sum(int count, ...)
{
    va_list ap;
    int total = 0;

    va_start(ap, count);
    while (count-- > 0)
        total += va_arg(ap, int);
    va_end(ap);
    return total;
}

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", sum(4, 1, 2, 3, 4));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Functions} {vprintf} {printf}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic auto
@keys auto
 auto

 Storage class of local variables.

## Syntax

   auto type name;

## Description

 Local variables declared inside a function are automatic by default,
 so auto is almost never written. An automatic variable lives on the
 stack, exists while its block runs and has no defined value until it
 is assigned. auto is not allowed at file scope.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    auto int count = 3;      /* same as: int count = 3; */

    while (count > 0)
        printf("%d ", count--);
    printf("liftoff\n");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {static} {register} {extern} {Storage classes and scope}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic break
@keys break
 break

 Leaves the innermost loop or switch.

## Syntax

   break;

## Description

 break jumps to the first statement after the closest enclosing for,
 while, do or switch statement. Using break outside of these is an
 error. To leave several nested loops use goto or a flag.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
        if (i * i > 50)
            break;           /* leaves the for loop */
    }
    printf("first i with i*i > 50 is %d\n", i);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {continue} {switch} {for} {while} {do} {goto}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic case
@keys case
 case

 Labels a statement inside a switch.

## Syntax

   case constant-expression:

## Description

 The value of the switch expression is compared with each case label.
 The label must be an integer constant expression and no two labels of
 one switch may have the same value. Execution continues with the
 labelled statement and runs on into the next label unless it is
 stopped with break.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int c;

    for (c = 'a'; c <= 'f'; c++) {
        switch (c) {
        case 'a': case 'e':
            printf("%c is a vowel\n", c);
            break;
        default:
            printf("%c is a consonant\n", c);
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {switch} {default} {break}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic char
@keys char
 char

 Character type, 1 byte.

## Syntax

   char name;
   signed char name;
   unsigned char name;

## Description

 char holds one byte and is also used as a small integer. Plain char
 is signed unless Options|Compiler|Code generation|Unsigned characters
 is on (fcc -K). A character constant such as 'a' has type int. Arrays
 of char hold strings.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    char letter = 'A';
    unsigned char byte = 200;

    printf("%c = %d\n", letter, letter);
    printf("byte = %d, size %d\n", byte, (int)sizeof byte);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {short} {int} {unsigned} {signed}
 {Character constants and escape sequences} {Strings}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic const
@keys const
 const

 Marks an object as read-only.

## Syntax

   const type name = value;
   const type *pointer;      /* data is read-only */
   type *const pointer;      /* pointer is read-only */

## Description

 Assigning to a const object, or through a pointer to const, is the
 error 'Cannot modify a const object'. A const object must get its
 value when it is declared. const is checked for scalars, pointers and
 arrays; it is not tracked for whole struct or union objects (their
 const members are).

 Functions that only read through a pointer should take a pointer to
 const; string literals are best held in const char pointers.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

static int length(const char *s)
{
    int n = 0;
    while (*s++)             /* the string is only read */
        n++;
    return n;
}

int main(void)
{
    const int limit = 10;
    const char *text = "const";

    printf("%d %d\n", limit, length(text));
    /* limit = 5;   would be: Cannot modify a const object */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {volatile} {Pointers} {Type qualifiers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic continue
@keys continue
 continue

 Starts the next iteration of a loop.

## Syntax

   continue;

## Description

 continue skips the rest of the loop body. In a while or do loop
 control goes to the test; in a for loop it goes to the third
 expression (the step) and then to the test. It is an error outside of
 a loop.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        if (i % 2 == 0)
            continue;        /* skip even numbers */
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {break} {for} {while} {do}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic default
@keys default
 default

 Label for the cases a switch does not list.

## Syntax

   default:

## Description

 If no case label matches the switch expression, execution continues
 at the default label. A switch has at most one default label and it
 may appear anywhere among the cases.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

static const char *name(int day)
{
    switch (day) {
    case 6: case 7: return "weekend";
    default:        return "weekday";
    }
}

int main(void)
{
    printf("%s %s\n", name(3), name(7));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {switch} {case} {break}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic do
@keys do
 do

 Loop that tests its condition after the body.

## Syntax

   do statement while (expression);

## Description

 The body always runs at least once. The loop repeats while the
 expression is nonzero. Note the semicolon after the closing
 parenthesis.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n = 1234, digits = 0;

    do {
        digits++;
        n /= 10;
    } while (n != 0);
    printf("%d digits\n", digits);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {while} {for} {break} {continue}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic double
@keys double
 double

 Double precision floating-point type, 8 bytes.

## Syntax

   double name;

## Description

 IEEE 754 64-bit value with about 15 decimal digits. All floating-
 point arithmetic is done in double precision. long double is the same
 type as double. Floating constants without a suffix are double.
 printf prints a double with %f %e or %g and scanf reads it with %lf.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    double radius = 2.5;
    double area = 3.14159265358979 * radius * radius;

    printf("area = %.4f\n", area);
    printf("size = %d bytes\n", (int)sizeof(double));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {float} {Floating constants} {printf formats}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic else
@keys else
 else

 Alternative branch of an if statement.

## Syntax

   if (expression) statement else statement

## Description

 An else belongs to the nearest preceding if that has no else. Use
 braces to make another grouping.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n;

    for (n = -1; n <= 1; n++) {
        if (n < 0)
            printf("negative\n");
        else if (n == 0)
            printf("zero\n");
        else
            printf("positive\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {if} {switch} {Conditional operator}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic enum
@keys enum
 enum

 Defines a set of named integer constants.

## Syntax

   enum tag { name, name = value, ... };
   enum tag variable;

## Description

 Constants without a value are one more than the previous one; the
 first is 0. The constants have type int and can be used wherever an
 integer constant is allowed. An enum variable is an int, or with
 Options|Compiler|Code generation|Treat enums as ints turned off (fcc
 -b-) the smallest of char, short and int that holds all values.

 A trailing comma after the last constant is accepted.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

enum color { RED, GREEN = 5, BLUE };

int main(void)
{
    enum color c = BLUE;

    printf("RED=%d GREEN=%d BLUE=%d\n", RED, GREEN, BLUE);
    printf("c is %s\n", c == BLUE ? "blue" : "other");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {typedef} {Integer types} {switch}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic extern
@keys extern
 extern

 Declares an object or function defined elsewhere.

## Syntax

   extern type name;
   extern type function(parameters);

## Description

 extern describes a name without creating it; the definition must
 exist in another source file of the program or in the library,
 otherwise the linker reports 'Undefined symbol'. Functions have
 external linkage by default, so extern is optional in their
 declarations. Several files given to fcc, or listed in a project,
 form one program.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int counter = 0;             /* the definition */

static void bump(void)
{
    extern int counter;      /* refers to the global above */
    counter++;
}

int main(void)
{
    bump(); bump();
    printf("counter = %d\n", counter);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {static} {auto} {Storage classes and scope} {Functions}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic float
@keys float
 float

 Single precision floating-point type, 4 bytes.

## Syntax

   float name;

## Description

 IEEE 754 32-bit value with about 7 decimal digits. Arithmetic is
 performed in double precision and the result is rounded to float when
 it is stored. Function arguments of type float are passed as double.
 Use the suffix f for a float constant.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    float third = 1.0f / 3.0f;

    printf("%.10f\n", third);
    printf("size = %d bytes\n", (int)sizeof(float));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {double} {Floating constants}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic for
@keys for
 for

 Loop with initialisation, test and step.

## Syntax

   for (expression1; expression2; expression3) statement
   for (declaration; expression2; expression3) statement

## Description

 expression1 runs once, then the loop repeats while expression2 is
 nonzero; expression3 runs after each pass. Any of the three may be
 left out; a missing test is always true. A variable declared in the
 first part is only visible in the loop.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i, sum = 0;

    for (i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
        sum += i;
    printf("sum = %d\n", sum);

    for (;;) {               /* endless loop */
        if (sum-- < 50) break;
    }
    printf("sum = %d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {while} {do} {break} {continue}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic goto
@keys goto
 goto

 Jumps to a label in the same function.

## Syntax

   goto label;
   label: statement

## Description

 A label is an identifier followed by a colon. Labels have function
 scope, so the jump can go forward or backward but never into another
 function. goto is useful for leaving several nested loops or for
 common clean-up code.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i, j;

    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
        for (j = 0; j < 10; j++)
            if (i * j == 42)
                goto found;
    printf("not found\n");
    return 1;
found:
    printf("%d * %d = 42\n", i, j);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {break} {continue} {return}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic if
@keys if
 if

 Conditional execution.

## Syntax

   if (expression) statement
   if (expression) statement else statement

## Description

 The statement runs when the expression is nonzero. The expression
 must have a scalar type (integer, floating or pointer). A pointer is
 true when it is not NULL. With -wcln the compiler warns when the
 condition is a constant, with -wasn (Possibly incorrect assignment)
 when it is an assignment such as if (a = b).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int year = 2024;

    if (year % 4 == 0 && (year % 100 != 0 || year % 400 == 0))
        printf("%d is a leap year\n", year);
    else
        printf("%d is not a leap year\n", year);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {else} {switch} {Relational and logical operators}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic int
@keys int
 int

 Integer type, 4 bytes.

## Syntax

   int name;
   signed int name;
   unsigned int name;

## Description

 int is 32 bits in Flash C (it was 16 bits in DOS compilers), range
 -2147483648 to 2147483647; unsigned int is 0 to 4294967295. Integer
 arithmetic is done in int or, if an operand is long, in long.
 Overflow wraps around.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>

int main(void)
{
    int big = INT_MAX;
    unsigned int u = 4000000000u;

    printf("%d %u\n", big, u);
    printf("size = %d bytes\n", (int)sizeof(int));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {short} {long} {char} {unsigned} {signed} {Integer types}
 {Type conversions}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic long
@keys long
 long

 Long integer type, 8 bytes.

## Syntax

   long name;
   unsigned long name;

## Description

 long is 64 bits in Flash C, range -9223372036854775808 to
 9223372036854775807. Pointers are the same size. long long is
 accepted and means long. Constants get the suffix l or L; printf uses
 %ld.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    long f = 1;
    int i;

    for (i = 2; i <= 20; i++)
        f *= i;
    printf("20! = %ld\n", f);
    printf("size = %d bytes\n", (int)sizeof(long));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {int} {short} {unsigned} {Integer constants} {Integer types}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic main
@keys main argc argv envp
 main

 The function where the program starts.

## Syntax

   int main(void)
   int main(int argc, char **argv)
   int main(int argc, char **argv, char **envp)

## Description

 argc is the number of command-line words including the program name,
 argv[0..argc-1] are the words, argv[argc] is NULL. envp is the
 environment as NAME=value strings; the global environ holds the same.
 Set the arguments for Ctrl+F9 in Run|Arguments. The value returned
 from main (or passed to exit) is the exit code of the program;
 falling off the end gives 0. #pragma startup functions run before
 main, #pragma exit functions after it. Options|Libraries|Wildcard
 arguments expands * and ?.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    int i;

    printf("%d argument(s)\n", argc - 1);
    for (i = 1; i < argc; i++)
        printf("  %s\n", argv[i]);
    return argc > 1 ? 0 : 1;
}
@end

## See also

 {return} {exit} {Libraries and linking}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic register
@keys register
 register

 Asks that a variable be kept in a register.

## Syntax

   register type name;

## Description

 register is a hint. Flash C does not allocate registers, so the
 keyword is accepted and has no effect on the generated code. It is
 only allowed for local variables and parameters; at file scope it is
 an error.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    register int i;
    long sum = 0;

    for (i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
        sum += i;
    printf("%ld\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {auto} {static} {Storage classes and scope}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic return
@keys return
 return

 Leaves a function, optionally with a value.

## Syntax

   return;
   return expression;

## Description

 The expression is converted to the return type of the function.
 Falling off the end of a function that should return a value gives
 the warning 'Function should return a value' and a result of 0. A
 void function must not return a value ('Void functions may not return
 a value'). Returning from main ends the program with that exit code.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

static int max(int a, int b)
{
    if (a > b)
        return a;
    return b;
}

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", max(3, 9));
    return 0;                /* exit code 0 */
}
@end

## See also

 {Functions} {void} {main} {goto}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic short
@keys short
 short

 Short integer type, 2 bytes.

## Syntax

   short name;
   short int name;
   unsigned short name;

## Description

 short holds -32768 to 32767, unsigned short 0 to 65535. In
 expressions short values are first converted to int. printf uses %hd.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    short s = 32767;
    unsigned short u = 65535;

    s++;                     /* wraps to -32768 */
    printf("%d %u\n", s, u);
    printf("size = %d bytes\n", (int)sizeof(short));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {int} {long} {char} {Integer types}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic signed
@keys signed
 signed

 Integer type that can hold negative values.

## Syntax

   signed char name;
   signed int name;

## Description

 Integer types are signed by default; signed matters mainly for char,
 where plain char may be unsigned (fcc -K).


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    signed char c = -5;
    signed s = -100000;      /* signed alone means signed int */

    printf("%d %d\n", c, s);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {unsigned} {char} {int}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic sizeof
@keys sizeof
 sizeof

 Size of a type or object in bytes.

## Syntax

   sizeof expression
   sizeof (type)

## Description

 The result is a compile-time constant of type unsigned long (size_t).
 The expression is not evaluated. sizeof an array gives the size of
 the whole array, which is the usual way to find the number of
 elements. sizeof cannot be applied to a bit field, a function or an
 incomplete type.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int table[10];
    char *p = 0;

    printf("int %d, long %d, pointer %d\n", (int)sizeof(int),
        (int)sizeof(long), (int)sizeof p);
    printf("elements: %d\n", (int)(sizeof table / sizeof table[0]));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Data types} {Arrays} {Pointers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic static
@keys static
 static

 Limits the scope of a name or keeps a local alive.

## Syntax

   static type name = constant;
   static type function(parameters) { ... }

## Description

 At file scope static gives a variable or function internal linkage:
 it can only be used in its own source file. Inside a function static
 creates a variable that keeps its value between calls; it is
 initialised once, before the program starts (to 0 if there is no
 initialiser), and the initialiser must be a constant.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

static int next_id(void)
{
    static int id = 100;     /* kept between calls */
    return id++;
}

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d %d %d\n", next_id(), next_id(), next_id());
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {extern} {auto} {Storage classes and scope}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic struct
@keys struct
 struct

 Groups variables of different types.

## Syntax

   struct tag { type member; ... };
   struct tag variable;
   variable.member   pointer->member

## Description

 Members are stored in order and padded to their natural alignment
 (Options|Compiler|Code generation|Align structure members; with it
 off, fcc -a-, there is no padding). A structure can be assigned,
 passed to a function and returned by value; the warning 'Structure
 passed by value' is available (-wstv). A structure cannot contain
 itself but may contain a pointer to its own type. Members can be bit
 fields.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

struct person {
    char name[20];
    int age;
};

static void birthday(struct person *p)
{
    p->age++;
}

int main(void)
{
    struct person who;

    strcpy(who.name, "Ann");
    who.age = 30;
    birthday(&who);
    printf("%s is %d (struct is %d bytes)\n", who.name, who.age,
        (int)sizeof who);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {union} {typedef} {Member access operators} {Bit fields} {Initializers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic switch
@keys switch
 switch

 Multi-way branch on an integer value.

## Syntax

   switch (expression) statement

## Description

 The expression must have an integer type. Control goes to the case
 label with the same value, or to default, or past the switch when
 there is neither. Without break execution falls through to the next
 label. Declarations are not allowed directly after a label.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n;

    for (n = 1; n <= 4; n++) {
        switch (n) {
        case 1: printf("one ");
        case 2: printf("two ");   /* case 1 falls through */
                break;
        case 3: printf("three ");
                break;
        default: printf("many ");
        }
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {case} {default} {break} {if}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic typedef
@keys typedef
 typedef

 Gives a new name to a type.

## Syntax

   typedef type name;

## Description

 A typedef declaration looks like a variable declaration with typedef
 in front; the name becomes a synonym for the type. Typedefs make
 structure and function pointer types readable. Repeating a typedef in
 the same scope is an error.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

typedef unsigned char byte;
typedef struct { int x, y; } point;
typedef int (*binop)(int, int);

static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }

int main(void)
{
    byte b = 255;
    point p = { 3, 4 };
    binop f = add;

    printf("%d %d %d\n", b, p.x + p.y, f(1, 2));
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {struct} {enum} {Function pointers}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic union
@keys union
 union

 Overlays several members in the same storage.

## Syntax

   union tag { type member; ... };

## Description

 All members start at the same address and the union is as large as
 its largest member. Only the member that was stored last holds a
 meaningful value. A union can be used to look at the same bytes as
 different types.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

union word {
    unsigned int whole;
    unsigned char byte[4];
};

int main(void)
{
    union word w;

    w.whole = 0x01020304;
    printf("%d %d %d %d (size %d)\n", w.byte[0], w.byte[1],
        w.byte[2], w.byte[3], (int)sizeof w);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {struct} {typedef} {Member access operators}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic unsigned
@keys unsigned
 unsigned

 Integer type without negative values.

## Syntax

   unsigned char name;
   unsigned short name;
   unsigned int name;
   unsigned long name;

## Description

 unsigned alone means unsigned int. Arithmetic on unsigned values
 wraps around modulo 2^N. When a signed and an unsigned operand of the
 same size are mixed, the signed one is converted to unsigned, which
 can make a comparison such as -1 < 1u false.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    unsigned int u = 0;

    u--;                     /* wraps around */
    printf("%u\n", u);
    printf("%d\n", -1 < 1u);   /* 0: -1 becomes unsigned */
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {signed} {int} {long} {Type conversions}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic void
@keys void
 void

 No value / no type.

## Syntax

   void function(void);
   void *pointer;

## Description

 As a return type void means the function returns nothing. In a
 parameter list (void) means no parameters, which is different from ()
 that declares nothing about the parameters. A void * can hold the
 address of any object and is converted to and from other pointer
 types without a cast; it cannot be dereferenced or used in pointer
 arithmetic.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

static void hello(void)
{
    printf("hello\n");
}

int main(void)
{
    void *block = malloc(16);      /* void * converts to any pointer */
    int *p = block;

    *p = 7;
    hello();
    printf("%d\n", *p);
    free(block);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {Functions} {Pointers} {return}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic volatile
@keys volatile
 volatile

 Marks an object that may change unexpectedly.

## Syntax

   volatile type name;

## Description

 volatile tells the compiler that a variable can change outside the
 normal flow of the program, for example in a signal handler. Flash C
 reads and writes variables exactly as written in the program and does
 not keep them in registers between statements, so the qualifier is
 accepted and the code is the same.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <signal.h>

static volatile int got_signal = 0;

static void handler(int sig)
{
    got_signal = 1;
}

int main(void)
{
    signal(SIGINT, handler);
    raise(SIGINT);
    printf("got_signal = %d\n", got_signal);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {const} {Type qualifiers} {signal}

 Back to {Language topics}

@topic while
@keys while
 while

 Loop that tests its condition before the body.

## Syntax

   while (expression) statement

## Description

 The statement is repeated as long as the expression is nonzero; it
 may not run at all. while (1) is an endless loop that is left with
 break, return or goto.


## Example

@example
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n = 27, steps = 0;

    while (n != 1) {
        n = (n % 2) ? 3 * n + 1 : n / 2;
        steps++;
    }
    printf("%d steps\n", steps);
    return 0;
}
@end

## See also

 {do} {for} {break} {continue}

 Back to {Language topics}
